WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.840
Peace be upon you and welcome to the fifth video of the second module in our fellowship.

00:05.120 --> 00:11.540
In this analytical explanation, we will experience a turning point that completely changes our way of thinking as health workers and educators.

00:11.640 --> 00:18.480
Without long introductions, let us enter directly into the topic through a famous allegorical story upon which public health pioneers across the world are raised.

00:18.920 --> 00:23.360
A story that takes us from mere rapid reaction to a far deeper strategy:

00:23.360 --> 00:29.660
Imagine with me this scene: A quiet day, and we are standing beside the bank of a deep river whose water is fast, rushing like a rocket.

00:29.900 --> 00:34.320
Suddenly, we hear a terrifying cry for help: a person is drowning, fighting the waves.

00:34.640 --> 00:39.660
Naturally, with pure human chivalry, you throw yourself into the water, pull the man out, and administer first aid.

00:39.880 --> 00:43.000
You barely took a breath when another cry comes from a second drowning person.

00:43.360 --> 00:47.360
You jumped and rescued him; then a third, fourth, fifth, and tenth.

00:48.320 --> 00:50.580
The situation turned into an exhausting marathon race.

00:50.580 --> 00:56.460
Doctors and rescuers depleted all their energy on the riverbank just to pull out the drowning people.

00:56.780 --> 01:01.620
This scene is precisely what occurs in our hospitals and emergency rooms when we remain chasing after reactions,

01:02.100 --> 01:04.480
until a wise person comes and asks the ingenious question:

01:04.840 --> 01:07.980
"Colleagues, why are we busy catching these drowning people?

01:07.980 --> 01:09.980
Who is throwing them into the water upstream in the first place?

01:09.980 --> 01:12.360
Why do we not go up to the river source and see what the story is?"

01:12.360 --> 01:18.000
And when they walked upstream, they found a broken bridge, with people falling through the hole one after another.

01:18.000 --> 01:25.000
The smart solution here was not to bring more divers and ambulances to the bottom of the river;

01:25.000 --> 01:30.000
the radical, intelligent solution was to repair the bridge upstream, install a barrier, and prevent people from falling in the first place.

01:30.000 --> 01:36.000
This allegorical story illustrates the golden rule in public health:

01:36.000 --> 01:42.000
Prevention before treatment, going upstream to the river source instead of exhausting ourselves downstream.

01:42.000 --> 01:50.000
In this video, we will learn how to transition our thinking from mere reactive treatment to proactive prevention.

01:50.000 --> 01:58.000
Let us look at the fundamental difference between treatment and prevention:

01:58.000 --> 02:05.000
Treatment is waiting for the person to get sick, collapse, enter the clinic, and then we begin treating and prescribing medication.

02:05.000 --> 02:15.300
While prevention refuses to let the disease occur in the first place.

02:15.300 --> 02:17.620
Prevention refuses to let the disease occur in the first place.

02:18.000 --> 02:23.040
And prevention is not just literary wisdom of the sort: "An ounce of prevention is better than a pound of cure";

02:23.260 --> 02:23.400
no,

02:23.760 --> 02:26.200
this is a strategy founded on four major goals:

02:26.520 --> 02:28.280
It reduces the burden and accumulation of diseases;

02:28.280 --> 02:31.620
improves quality of life so a person lives in health and productivity,

02:31.620 --> 02:32.780
not spending their life in pain;

02:33.080 --> 02:36.620
saves billions wasted in operating rooms, emergency departments, and pharmaceuticals;

02:37.580 --> 02:40.320
meaning a touch of prevention saves a lot of money;

02:40.620 --> 02:45.040
and builds a community with natural immunity capable of enduring before any epidemic or crisis.

02:45.460 --> 02:47.020
To implement this practically,

02:47.380 --> 02:50.620
public health experts divided prevention into three main levels:

02:51.100 --> 02:53.900
The first line of defense: Primary prevention.

02:54.260 --> 02:57.680
Here we shut the door completely in the face of disease before it touches the human body:

02:57.680 --> 03:04.500
such as vaccination programs, encouraging physical activity, sound nutrition, and awareness campaigns to prevent youth from smoking their first cigarette.

03:04.820 --> 03:07.160
Here we build a true preventive shield.

03:07.620 --> 03:09.720
The second line of defense: Secondary prevention.

03:10.040 --> 03:12.700
If disease manages to infiltrate silently without symptoms,

03:12.960 --> 03:16.400
our goal is to catch it in its earliest stages before it erupts and causes a crisis:

03:16.720 --> 03:20.140
such as periodic blood pressure screening to catch the silent killer before a stroke;

03:20.420 --> 03:23.060
mammograms for early detection of breast cancer;

03:23.060 --> 03:27.820
and HbA1c tests to catch the pre-diabetes stage and adjust the trajectory.

03:28.020 --> 03:30.500
The third line of defense: Tertiary prevention.

03:30.800 --> 03:34.580
If the disease occurred, established itself, and became chronic, we do not raise the white flag:

03:34.860 --> 03:38.640
Our goal is to prevent complications and provide rehabilitation so the patient lives in the best possible form:

03:38.960 --> 03:42.340
such as cardiac rehabilitation programs after surgeries;

03:42.560 --> 03:46.960
rigorous follow-up for diabetic patients to protect kidneys, eyes, and limbs;

03:46.960 --> 03:51.240
and psychological support programs for patients to adapt and integrate into society.

03:51.240 --> 03:54.140
Do you know who the real dynamo is across these three levels?

03:54.140 --> 03:55.300
It is the health educator:

03:55.600 --> 03:58.640
In primary, educating people on sound healthy habits;

03:58.800 --> 04:03.460
in secondary, removing fear and hesitation from their hearts and encouraging them to screen early;

04:03.600 --> 04:08.720
in tertiary, standing beside the chronic patient and simplifying how to organize their medications and live positively.

04:09.100 --> 04:13.260
The health educator is the magical tool transforming plans into lived reality.

04:13.260 --> 04:16.460
Now, how do we apply this to climate crises?

04:16.460 --> 04:18.840
Imagine before us a harsh, scorching heatwave:

04:19.140 --> 04:25.840
The treatment pathway: We sit in the emergency room waiting for the elderly and construction workers to collapse from heat stress to give them fluids and injections.

04:26.180 --> 04:28.780
The prevention pathway: We act two days before the wave:

04:29.120 --> 04:32.120
Going down to the street and educating people to drink water before feeling thirsty,

04:32.440 --> 04:37.440
obligating employers to halt work during peak sun hours, and checking on the elderly in their homes.

04:37.440 --> 04:41.140
Certainly, proactive action protects hundreds of lives before they fall ill.

04:41.140 --> 04:43.660
Climate is not an illness to which we give an antibiotic and it recovers;

04:43.980 --> 04:46.140
climate changes the rules of the entire game,

04:46.400 --> 04:49.580
and prevention is the weapon of adaptation and building community resilience.

04:49.980 --> 04:52.400
All this talk is summarized by the public health mindset.

04:52.720 --> 04:54.940
Let us apply it to a challenge like childhood obesity:

04:55.280 --> 04:59.300
The ordinary clinical mindset says: Open weight-loss clinics and treat child by child;

04:59.300 --> 05:03.940
important, but that is after the problem occurred, the child became overweight, and unable to move.

05:04.260 --> 05:06.780
Whereas the upstream river mindset goes down to schools,

05:06.780 --> 05:09.700
bans the sale of chips and sodas in cafeterias,

05:09.800 --> 05:11.180
increases physical education classes,

05:11.500 --> 05:14.260
and trains parents on healthy, economical home meals.

05:14.420 --> 05:17.620
Here, we protect an entire generation from the trap of obesity and its illnesses.

05:18.080 --> 05:20.640
True health leaders always hold three questions in mind:

05:21.000 --> 05:22.700
How do we prevent the problem from its roots?

05:22.700 --> 05:24.720
How do we detect it early in its infancy?

05:24.720 --> 05:27.760
And how do we minimize its effects and complications in the community?

05:27.760 --> 05:30.740
The conclusion: Treatment saves individuals after they fall ill;

05:30.740 --> 05:34.560
yet strong communities are built on proactive prevention and early detection.

05:34.960 --> 05:39.220
Your role as a health educator begins in the field before disease even knocks on people's doors.

05:39.560 --> 05:42.900
In our upcoming exploration in the sixth video, we will answer the decisive question:

05:43.340 --> 05:45.820
How do we know what the real health problems are

05:45.820 --> 05:48.980
that pain our community, without guessing from our heads?

05:48.980 --> 05:52.940
We will learn the golden tool: Community health needs assessment,

05:53.220 --> 05:54.960
and listen to the true voice of the field.

05:54.960 --> 05:58.280
Before we conclude, we leave you with an applied activity:

06:00.740 --> 06:04.740
Diabetes, smoking, childhood obesity, or heat stress:

06:05.100 --> 06:06.620
Identify in your notebook:

06:07.000 --> 06:09.740
What is the action that represents primary prevention to stop it?

06:09.740 --> 06:13.520
What screening represents secondary prevention to detect it early?

06:13.520 --> 06:17.280
And what tertiary prevention program limits its complications?

06:17.280 --> 06:18.360
Thank you for your attention,

06:18.780 --> 06:19.940
and we will see you in the next video.
